54,3,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
55,3,1,talk,May,,"Looking at the candidates, the Night Service Extension (D) had a successful pilot, but @Benjamin's findings on the late-turn pool make it look impossible to staff.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"I agree, May. Plan D is a staffing disaster waiting to happen. Plan B also requires roadway crews we simply don't have.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,1,talk,Midori,,"Plan B's bus corridor might help the mid-city slowness, but if the crowds are clearing, maybe it's not the priority. Plan C's station upgrades sound helpful for movement.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, Plan C is very dangerous. The pre-war shells have those unrecorded voids I found. It would likely stall for weeks, just like previous jobs.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Plan A, the Integrated Fare Platform, addresses the inefficiency of having three separate contractors, which would help the trimmed technology budget.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,2,talk,May,,"Plan A is interesting, but @Midori found that ticketing isn't where the passengers lose the most time. Is it worth the investment?","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,It is certainly more feasible than D or B. It doesn't require a massive increase in roadway crews or a depleted late-turn pool.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,Midori,,"If we can't do C because of the shells, and B and D are staffing nightmares, Plan A is the most harmonious choice, even if it's not the primary passenger pain point.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"I agree. Plan A avoids the physical risks of the pre-war stations and the bureaucratic mess of the joint operators' committee, as it's a system change.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,Plan A aligns with the financial reality. It streamlines contractor fees and uses the technology line more effectively. It is the most sustainable option.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,3,talk,May,,I'm convinced. It's better to have a functioning fare system than a night service that can't be staffed or a station project that hits a void.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,The logic is sound. It is the only plan that doesn't assume the appearance of non-existent staff. I vote for Plan A.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,Midori,,I agree. It's the safest and most balanced approach given our current constraints. I vote for Plan A.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Shizue,,I also vote for Plan A. It avoids the hidden traps in the ground and the lack of cooperation between operators.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,I concur. It addresses the contractor inefficiency and fits our budget constraints. I vote for Plan A.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,4,talk,May,,I'll join you all. It's the most pragmatic choice. I vote for Plan A.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
